[
 {
  "verse_id": "chunk-d-memory-pramana-v4",
  "song": "chunk-d-memory-pramana",
  "verse": 4,
  "scope": "passage",
  "structured": true,
  "speaker": "Abhinavagupta (commentator voice)",
  "question": "How can the self be the *eternal* knower, if memory reaches only what one has\nexperienced — and the Rāmāyaṇa was composed by Vālmīki long ago, whom \"I\" was not? The objection: \"I\nwas not at the time of the Rāmāyaṇa.\" Abhinavagupta answers that the *param-essence knower* — the\nconsciousness-self — is un-cut by all three times; the \"I\" that was not at that time is only the\npresent body, not the self. Memory itself, he shows, presupposes the eternal knower.",
  "premises": [
   "If memory reaches only the experienced, then \"I was not at the time of the Rāmāyaṇa\" — for that time\nwas not experienced by me. If it is said \"from the āgama we know that time,\" then the āgama too\npresupposes the experiencer's experience. But Vālmīki was the experiencer then, not \"I.\" The question\nis apt; but the *aham-vimarśa* (the \"I\"-recollection) of the consciousness-nature is established as\neternal, to this extent. And he is the one param-essence knower; if the other-breaths-minds-\neight-fold-city-bodies are distinct, the knower-distinction is made by their difference. So the \"this\nbody was not at that time\" is the truth here [not \"I was not\"]. At the dissolution and the beginning of\ncreation, \"we were not\" — else the special-time-reference would not be. In the \"we were not\" cognition,\nthe past-time-appearance, brought into the liṭ (perfect) ending, does flash. This is a cognition; and\nit is not the experience-form (else, like the yogin, the present would flash the past-object).\nHence it is memory; and memory presupposes the prior experience. The utprekṣā (conjecture) too\npresupposes memory or experience at the root. But the pure consciousness-mere, being free of the\ntime-tint and incapable of appearing as a thing beyond its own form, cannot [alone] bear the memory —\nso the memory is [of] the body-and-the-rest-tinted. The prior-body-tint is distinct from this-body-\ntint; so the memory-ness is not clear, like the young man who was not a child (the \"not-yet-child\"\nstate).",
   "Where the conceit of the experiencer is firm in the body, there the memory-ness is clear. Enough: it is\nestablished that the self's manifestation is not un-precedented (apūrva). The apūrvatva is by being;\nthe apūrva-prakāśatva by the attainment. The self's non-being-before, nor the experience-of-the-\nexistent — in truth, the being-ness rests in the manifestation, so the being-priority rests in the\nmanifestation's un-precedented-ness — hence the sāmānādhikaraṇya (co-reference) is fit. The \"cakāra\" is\nnot used. The knower's apūrva-prakāśatva-dealing is not without the prior knower (it needs the\nmemory of the prior time); without it, the being's pervader (the non-dependence on that form) would\nfail — like the cloth without the clay-lump. So the knower's apūrva-prakāśatva presupposes the\neternal knower. But the param-essence knower is never apūrva-prakāśa; and all the knowers are\nun-divided from him — so in truth there is no apūrva-prakāśatā. (The Gītā 2.12: \"for truly I was\nnever not… nor these lords; nor shall we not be\" — the self, the param-essence knower, is un-cut by\nthe three times; by its un-dividedness, so are the others.) In the māyā-knower, the apūrva-prakāśatā\nmay be, but it rests in the eternal param-essence knower. The pramāṇa/karaṇa is thus grounded in the\neternal knower."
  ],
  "inference": "Decision for L2\nProceed. The argument is understood: memory presupposes the prior experience, and hence the eternal\nknower; the \"I was not at the Rāmāyaṇa\" refers only to the present body, not the self; the\nparam-essence knower is un-cut by all three times (the Gītā 2.12), and all the knowers are un-divided\nfrom him — so in truth there is no un-precedented manifestation. The pramāṇa is grounded in the\neternal knower.",
  "uncertainties": [
   "The Gītā 2.12 citation (the \"I was never not\") — the load-bearing confirmation; cited, the exact",
   "The \"atiśiśutā-padānabhūta iva yūnaḥ\" (the young man who was not yet a child) — the memory-"
  ],
